{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MV8CVyctbdXqb2zU3beqRz","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Yamale schema for the architectural spec (path from\n2\t# `documents.architecture_yaml` in true-bdd.yaml, conventionally\n3\t# docs/architecture/architecture.yaml).\n4\t#\n5\t# The filename is not free: the schema gate pairs\n6\t# `true-bdd/<key>-schema.yaml` with `documents.<key>`, and the key is\n7\t# `architecture_yaml`. A file named `architecture-schema.yaml` would\n8\t# resolve no document and fail the gate rather than validate anything.\n9\t#\n10\t#   yamale -s true-bdd/architecture_yaml-schema.yaml docs/architecture/architecture.yaml\n11\t#\n12\t# The engine decodes only `testing:`, `services[].{name,path,language,writable,libraries,public_interface}`\n13\t# and, for the `D: term-mappings` check, `terms:`; everything else here is\n14\t# spec a host writes for its own readers and for the checklists' prompts.\n15\t# Which is exactly why it needs a schema: nothing else would notice a typo\n16\t# in it.\n17\t#\n18\t# Yamale is strict by default — an unlisted key is an error — so a host\n19\t# adding a block to its architectural spec adds it here too.\n20\t\n21\tarchitecture: include('architecture')\n22\t# No `vocabulary:` here: the product document holds it and its catalogue,\n23\t# which `us refine` cites; this document only maps the words (`terms:`).\n24\t\n25\t---\n26\t# One `testing:` section for the whole repository, beside the services it\n27\t# exercises: it says how the tests run, once. Which service a test belongs\n28\t# to and which file it lives in are the scenario's, not a suite's.\n29\tarchitecture:\n30\t  testing: include('testing')\n31\t  services: list(include('service'), min=1)\n32\t  terms: list(include('term_mapping'), required=False)\n33\t  prompt_data: map(include('prompt_data'), required=False)\n34\t\n35\t---\n36\t# How one product glossary word turns into the implementation, and how a\n37\t# step definition